1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,240
I'm Abhishek. This is Whodunit, built for

2
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:04,880
the Agents of SigNoz hackathon —

3
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:07,040
Track 2, Signals and Dashboards.

4
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:09,720
When production breaks, every observability

5
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:11,740
tool shows you the difference between the

6
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:14,660
traces that failed and the ones that didn't.

7
00:00:15,230 --> 00:00:17,700
BubbleUp, Trace Patterns, Grafana's compare

8
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:20,300
— they rank attributes and hand you a panel.

9
00:00:20,940 --> 00:00:23,160
Then a human re-types that finding into a

10
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:24,840
query, at three in the morning.

11
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:26,980
Whodunit closes that loop.

12
00:00:27,420 --> 00:00:29,780
One ClickHouse scan, mine the pattern that

13
00:00:29,820 --> 00:00:32,460
separates the cohorts, compile it into a

14
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:34,800
real SigNoz query, verify it against the

15
00:00:34,840 --> 00:00:37,520
live engine, and arm it as an alert.

16
00:00:37,810 --> 00:00:39,920
Five stages, no LLM anywhere.

17
00:00:44,220 --> 00:00:47,560
And this isn't a problem I invented for a

18
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:50,680
hackathon. SigNoz's co-founder asked for

19
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:54,770
exactly this, three and a half years ago —

20
00:00:54,920 --> 00:00:57,480
still open. Here's the fault, drawn.

21
00:00:58,540 --> 00:01:00,620
A healthy checkout calls the flag service.

22
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:03,940
A failing one retries redis, and the

23
00:01:04,240 --> 00:01:05,460
flag-service span is missing.

24
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:08,200
But the retry alone shows up in healthy

25
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:11,820
traces too. And plenty of healthy traces

26
00:01:11,860 --> 00:01:12,960
skip the flag check.

27
00:01:13,580 --> 00:01:15,600
Neither condition alone separates them —

28
00:01:15,640 --> 00:01:20,320
only both at once. One question to

29
00:01:20,820 --> 00:01:22,600
ClickHouse. Then the whole lattice is

30
00:01:22,640 --> 00:01:23,860
enumerated locally.

31
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:27,840
The machine weighs the obvious answers —

32
00:01:28,060 --> 00:01:29,820
the edge alone — and rejects them.

33
00:01:30,240 --> 00:01:32,460
Only the conjunction separates the cohorts,

34
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:35,330
at thirteen times lift.

35
00:01:36,180 --> 00:01:38,633
This is what every flat tool sees —

36
00:01:38,907 --> 00:01:40,880
BubbleUp, compare, Trace Patterns.

37
00:01:41,490 --> 00:01:44,460
Its best single predicate lands at precision

38
00:01:44,864 --> 00:01:46,256
zero point one seven.

39
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:48,840
The fault needs two conditions at once.

40
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:50,720
No model wrote this.

41
00:01:50,820 --> 00:01:53,530
It's compiled into SigNoz's own grammar —

42
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:56,020
A implies B, and not C —

43
00:01:56,240 --> 00:01:57,680
then run back against the engine.

44
00:01:58,180 --> 00:02:01,400
Mined sixty-one. SigNoz returned sixty-one.

45
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:04,340
And this is why it has to be SigNoz.

46
00:02:05,060 --> 00:02:10,060
Traces and logs live in one ClickHouse, so a

47
00:02:12,460 --> 00:02:12,860
single scan joins them by trace ID.

48
00:02:12,460 --> 00:02:16,180
On Tempo plus Loki, that join doesn't exist.

49
00:02:16,940 --> 00:02:18,760
That permalink opens the machine-written

50
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:21,600
query in SigNoz's own Trace Explorer —

51
00:02:21,840 --> 00:02:24,600
all three leaves and the operator, rendered

52
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:28,400
in the real UI. The result set collapses to

53
00:02:28,440 --> 00:02:29,540
the matched traces.

54
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:32,940
Open one, and the flame graph shows the

55
00:02:32,980 --> 00:02:36,300
shape: payment retries redis, and

56
00:02:36,580 --> 00:02:37,780
flag-service never ran.

57
00:02:44,380 --> 00:02:47,660
One flag arms it. The discriminator becomes

58
00:02:47,700 --> 00:02:52,020
a native v2alpha1 alert with warning and

59
00:02:52,060 --> 00:02:53,160
critical thresholds.

60
00:02:53,810 --> 00:02:57,413
Replay the fault, and at t plus one

61
00:02:57,667 --> 00:03:01,540
eighty-two seconds the webhook fires —

62
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:04,540
the rule ID in that payload is the rule the

63
00:03:04,580 --> 00:03:05,680
command just created.

64
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:08,600
Everyone else in this category stops at a

65
00:03:08,640 --> 00:03:13,640
panel. None of them hand you back a query, a

66
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:15,340
dashboard, and an armed tripwire.

67
00:03:15,380 --> 00:03:16,540
Here's what matters more.

68
00:03:16,940 --> 00:03:18,327
Point it at a decoy —

69
00:03:18,373 --> 00:03:21,040
an attribute that tracks failure but doesn't

70
00:03:21,080 --> 00:03:22,820
cause it — and it abstains.

71
00:03:23,340 --> 00:03:26,300
Point it at a healthy system, it abstains

72
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:30,640
again. Across six seeded faults, it never

73
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:31,940
named a false culprit.

74
00:03:31,980 --> 00:03:36,980
And this one it first got wrong —

75
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:40,780
it abstained where a real answer existed.

76
00:03:42,300 --> 00:03:44,920
I found the seam, fixed it, and left the

77
00:03:44,960 --> 00:03:47,260
original failure in the repo.

78
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:51,120
Run it twice. Same input, same seed, same

79
00:03:51,160 --> 00:03:53,840
verdict hash. There is no LLM anywhere in

80
00:03:53,880 --> 00:03:55,900
the runtime — this is deterministic.

81
00:03:56,980 --> 00:03:59,100
The corpus is synthetic and disclosed;

82
00:04:00,960 --> 00:04:02,860
ground truth comes from a manifest.

83
00:04:13,720 --> 00:04:16,780
And you don't need my machine to check any

84
00:04:16,820 --> 00:04:20,240
of it. The whole run is stepped through in

85
00:04:20,280 --> 00:04:23,160
the browser — the board, the compiled query,

86
00:04:23,900 --> 00:04:25,920
the receipt, the hash —

87
00:04:25,960 --> 00:04:27,440
hosted, no install.

88
00:04:30,920 --> 00:04:33,580
Everyone can show you the difference.

89
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:35,660
Only SigNoz can arm it.

90
00:04:36,420 --> 00:04:38,020
Everything's on GitHub —

91
00:04:38,180 --> 00:04:40,460
one command reproduces the whole run,

92
00:04:40,960 --> 00:04:42,820
casting file and lock committed.
